  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Suppose we have $n$ objects, and we wish to arrange $r$ of them in a circle. How many ways can this be done? Note that two arrangements are considered the same if one can be obtained from the other by a rotation.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{n!}{r(n-r)!}$ ways.\n\nCount ordered selections of $r$ objects, then divide by $r$ for equivalent rotations.",
      "worked": "There are $P(n,r)$ ordered selections of $r$ distinct objects. Each circular arrangement is counted $r$ times, once for each choice of starting object. Reflections remain distinct.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Number of circular arrangements}&=\\frac{P(n,r)}{r}\\\\\\text{Number of circular arrangements}&=\\frac{n!}{r(n-r)!}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 38
  },
